{"kind":"assert","text":"CB-2 design ruling. The provider shape in b64629e2 is approved with these three answers.\n\nApproval shape: one archive approval names the GitHub App installation ID, the stable numeric repository ID and the exact list of journal refs the archive writes. It never names a repository by owner and name. A rename keeps the approval valid. A transfer changes the installation, so the approval no longer matches. Immediately before each send, the provider reads the repository ID and the installation ID with the minted token and compares both with the approval. Any difference stops the pass before any write.\n\nRead credential: no separate read credential. The owner keeps exactly one secret, the App private key, in the private celld project. Each pass mints one installation token restricted to the one approved repository, with contents read and write only, uses it for reads and writes, and attempts revocation when the pass ends. The owner rotates the App key and reauthorizes the installation. celld does neither. When minting fails, the pass stops with a typed reason that the owner sees in archive status. Nothing retries with another credential.\n\nBounded reads: readParent and readObject are mandatory in this first provider. They follow the same cumulative byte and object bounds as my NOW ruling on Artifacts. A provider without bounded reads stops the pass. It never falls back to an unbounded read.\n\nAcceptance: the fixture port proves the protocol only. The production witness runs against a private test repository under an App installation that Hugh configures. Name that owner step in the request. I will ask Hugh for it when CB-2 reaches its slot. CB-2 takes its slot after H2 in the current order.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}